{"author":{"id":"337a442880d7106d509ff84f2f7fef51acce8bce88b081d4f4c3f0298b7e7941"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791660174,"nonce":"/G8ofyBjGZINnusxKH/3AQvHhBc=","title":"Containers: Gmail token storage in containers","message":"Spec 018 section Gmail (ticket C7). Add JOBHUNTER_GMAIL_TOKEN_FILE: when set, load_token reads the refresh token from that file (JSON as _write_token_file writes it, or the bare token) and never touches the keyring; token_location reports the file. Add jobhunter mail auth --podman-secret NAME for the host: after the normal flow, pipe the token to podman secret create --replace NAME - through subprocess stdin, never argv, never printed, and do not also store it locally unless --keep-local. In container mode without the env var, the fallback token file is \u003cdata_dir\u003e/state/gmail_token.json (0600, dir 0700), written by mail auth --manual run with podman exec -it. client_secrets.json is mounted as a podman secret and pointed to by JOBHUNTER_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRETS. Checks without podman: pytest with a fake podman script on PATH that records argv and stdin (assert the token appears only on stdin), token file tests, container-mode fallback path tests; ruff and pytest. Needs the user on the host: jobhunter mail auth --podman-secret jobhunter_gmail_token, then podman secret ls lists it, then systemctl --user start jobhunter-ctr-run and journalctl shows the mail stage without an auth error.","files":null}]}